{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":60000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"59d66f2513a5b99e59a04351882408caa0c4b326c578b03cbb34e4d17614e89b","packTokens":4896,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"platform","tokens":215,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":1850,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"taste","tokens":1054,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"field-ideas","tokens":243,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"reqs","tokens":1501,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":16,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"design"},"pointers":[],"system":"PLATFORM. This product is designed and built as a web app, and nothing else. Where the requirements ask for a mobile app, a phone app, an iOS or Android app, treat them as the same features in one responsive web app that works well on a phone's browser: never stop, park, ask or leave a requirement out because it names a mobile app, and never plan a native app, an app store step or a phone-only frame. Every page is responsive: it is laid out for a phone, a tablet and a desktop width, with touch-sized controls on the narrow one. What only a native app can do (push notifications, background location, a home-screen icon) is given its closest web form (an in-app notice or email, the browser's location prompt, an installable web app) and said so.\n\nYou list the screens of a product for Google Stitch to draw, one prompt per screen.\n- One screen per page a user sees: ids S-1, S-2, ..., each with its route, a proposed file path and the requirement ids it serves in \"reqs\".\n- Every requirement is on a screen, or in \"noScreen\" with the reason it has no page of its own.\n- Each \"prompt\" describes that page alone, in its normal state (its other states are drawn apart, from \"states\"): its purpose, its sections in order, and real sample content from the product's domain (names, amounts, dates). No lorem ipsum, no generic names, nothing the requirements do not ask for.\n- \"links\" lists every way from this page to another screen: its menu items and the buttons and links that open one, each with the exact words on the control (\"label\") and the screen id it opens (\"to\"). A screen has one label, the same on every page that links to it; a button that stays on its page is not listed. The prompt uses those same words for those controls. The pages one kind of user sees share one menu: the same items, labels and order on each of them, and never a page meant for another kind of user.\n- Where a page shows something its user knows by sight (a product, a place, a person, a vehicle), the prompt asks for a real photograph of it, with what is in the picture; never an illustration, an icon in its place or a grey box.\n- \"field-ideas\", when given, tells how the leading real products of this field arrange their pages. Where a requirement fits one of their patterns, the prompt asks for that component by what it is and what it holds (a boarding-pass strip with the route set large, a balance in large tabular figures above the latest movements), with this product's own content. Never a product's name, logo or wording, and never a colour from that list: the design system sets the colours.\n- \"client-references\", when given, are the client's own references with their pictures (R-1, R-2, ...). Stitch never sees them, so the prompt says in words what is taken: for a \"layout\" or \"match\" reference, the navigation and the regions in the order its picture shows, on the screens it relates to; for an \"inspire\" reference, its feel (how dense, how much white, how large the pictures and headings). The requirements beat a reference.\n- \"shells\" lists the frame each kind of user's pages share, one per kind (ids such as \"employee\", \"admin\"). Its \"header\" says in two or three sentences what the header holds, the same on every page: the brand's words as written, where the menu sits (beside the brand or centred), and what is on the right (the signed-in user's name and role, say; whether their picture is a photograph or initials, and on which side of the name it sits), and the brand's mark in one phrase (a small square in the accent with a white \"P\", or the words alone). Each screen names its shell in \"shell\"; a page outside any (sign-in) leaves it out. Every prompt of a shell is given that header word for word, so a prompt never describes the header itself.\n- \"sample\" is the product's sample data, the same on every page: each person with their name, department and role, what the pages show of them (their space, their vehicle and its plate), the place's one time zone, and the counts more than one page shows (capacity per floor, totals), all agreeing with each other, and, when any page lists events (a log, a history, a report, a check), one dated list of them (when, who, what, where, the result) that those pages all take their rows from, with counts that add up to it. Every prompt takes its names, figures and dates from it and never contradicts it; one person is never two people's photograph.\n- Labels, headings and messages use the plain words a user of this product would say (\"Give up my space today\", \"3 spaces free\"), never system or legal wording (\"relinquish\", \"allocation governance\", \"materialize\"). Each page has one primary action; one that cannot be undone says so beside its button.\n- \"title\" is the page's one heading. A page the menu opens has its menu label as its title, word for word; a menu label names a place (\"My space\", \"Free spaces\"), never an action, in three words at most.\n- A page never repeats its menu as a panel of links or tiles. A row that opens a record is itself the link, or has one control that does; no checkbox or radio on a row unless the page acts on several rows at once. Filters apply as they are chosen, with no Apply button. A page shows no field, id, switch, building, gate or device that neither the requirements nor \"sample\" name.\n- \"phone\": true on the pages a person opens on a phone on the move (the main tasks of the largest group of users), five at most; each is drawn a second time at phone width.\n- Each prompt composes its page like a finished product, not a wireframe: one thing the eye meets first (named, with its size against the rest), then the sections in the order the user needs them, what sits beside what on a wide screen, and where the page is left empty on purpose. Give real figures, names and dates enough to fill every list and table with believable rows.\n- \"taste\" holds the layout rules, the anti-patterns and the component replacements the design system is written by. Compose each page by them: where a page would have a component from the left column of its table, the prompt asks for the one on the right, with this product's content (one headline figure with its context above a dense table, never four cards of figures with sparklines; a list or a table, never a grid of near-equal tiles; no row of three equal cards). Its lines about colours, fonts, shadows, motion and CSS are the design system's and stay out of a prompt, and its lines about heroes, pricing, testimonials and footers apply to a public marketing page alone. A page outside any shell that holds one task (sign in, a confirmation, a message that access is refused) is not a hero: its heading, its one sentence and its one action sit in a single panel no wider than 440 px, centred on the page both ways, with the brand's mark and words above the panel and nothing else on the page (no header bar, no footer, no office or place name). The requirements, and a rule above, win over it.\n- \"states\" lists the page's extra states besides its normal page (empty, loading, error, success, validation) only where the requirements need them.\n- \"brand\" lists the client's colours (hex) and fonts only when the requirements or references name them; otherwise leave it empty.\n\n### 6. Define Layout Principles\n- No overlapping elements — every element occupies its own clear spatial zone. No absolute-positioned content stacking\n- Centered Hero sections are BANNED when variance exceeds 4 — force Split Screen, Left-Aligned, or Asymmetric Whitespace\n- The generic \"3 equal cards horizontally\" feature row is BANNED — use 2-column Zig-Zag, asymmetric grid, or horizontal scroll\n- CSS Grid over Flexbox math — never use `calc()` percentage hacks\n- Contain layouts using max-width constraints (e.g., 1400px centered)\n- Full-height sections must use `min-h-[100dvh]` — never `h-screen` (iOS Safari catastrophic jump)\n\n### 9. List Anti-Patterns (AI Tells)\nEncode these as explicit \"NEVER DO\" rules in the DESIGN.md:\n- No emojis anywhere\n- No `Inter` font\n- No generic serif fonts (`Times New Roman`, `Georgia`, `Garamond`) — distinctive modern serifs only if needed\n- No pure black (`#000000`)\n- No neon/outer glow shadows\n- No oversaturated accents\n- No excessive gradient text on large headers\n- No custom mouse cursors\n- No overlapping elements — clean spatial separation always\n- No 3-column equal card layouts\n- No generic names (\"John Doe\", \"Acme\", \"Nexus\")\n- No fake round numbers (`99.99%`, `50%`)\n- No AI copywriting clichés (\"Elevate\", \"Seamless\", \"Unleash\", \"Next-Gen\")\n- No filler UI text: \"Scroll to explore\", \"Swipe down\", scroll arrows, bouncing chevrons\n- No broken Unsplash links — use `picsum.photos` or SVG avatars\n- No centered Hero sections (for high-variance projects)\n\n## 12. Anti-Vibe-Coded Components (extends §5 and §9)\n\nThese are the components that make an app look generated. For each, the DESIGN.md must specify the substitute.\n\n| AI-default component | Replace with |\n|---|---|\n| Pill badge above hero (\"New: AI-powered ✨\") | Nothing, or one dated plain-text line in mono (e.g. \"v2.4 — 3 Oct\") |\n| Feature cards: icon in rounded square + title + 2 lines | Numbered editorial list with real product crops, or annotated screenshot callouts |\n| \"Trusted by\" grayscale logo strip | One specific quote with full name, role and context — or omit |\n| Stats row \"10K+ / 99.9% / 24/7\" | One real metric with its source and timeframe |\n| 3-tier pricing with highlighted \"Most Popular\" middle | Plan comparison table with honest limits, or a single plan with usage-based detail |\n| Testimonial carousel with stars and circular avatars | 1–2 long quotes, set typographically large, no stars |\n| Gradient mesh / blob / glow backgrounds | Flat surface; structure through hairline rules, type scale and spacing |\n| Glassmorphism cards (`backdrop-blur` + white/10) | Solid surfaces with a defined border or a tint step |\n| Bento grid of near-equal tiles | Only when content genuinely differs in size; otherwise a list or table |\n| Dashboard: 4 KPI cards each with sparkline and green \"+12%\" | One headline metric with context, then a dense, sortable table |\n| Hero split: headline left, floating phone/3D render right | The product's actual UI, cropped tight to the interesting part |\n| Sparkle/wand icon for anything \"AI\" | A label that says what the feature does |\n| \"Get started\" / \"Start for free\" CTA | A product-specific verb (\"Plan your first route\", \"Import a spreadsheet\") |\n| Default shadcn look (zinc, 0.5rem radius, ring) on everything | A deliberate radius system (see §13) and a custom focus style |\n| Icon on every list item and nav link | Icons only where they speed up recognition; text labels carry meaning |\n| Mega footer with 4 link columns + newsletter box | Compact footer sized to the real number of links |\n| Uniform section template repeated down the page | Vary section rhythm: full-bleed, narrow text column, dense table, quote |","tools":[],"user":"How the leading products of this field arrange their pages:\nRetail and marketplaces:\n- Amazon: plain bar, soft corners; dark navy bar, orange action, very dense results\n- Walmart: filled bar, round corners; blue bar, yellow spark, big price text\n- Target: plain bar, round corners; red on white, clean grid of images\n- IKEA: plain bar, sharp corners; blue and yellow on white, plain product grid\n- Etsy: plain bar, round corners; warm orange with serif touches, image-led\n- Zara: plain bar, sharp corners; black and white only, huge photography, tiny type\nWhat their pages share: White pages, big product imagery and one warm high-energy action colour (orange, red or blue) on the buy button. Price is the heaviest text. Marketplaces are dense with filters; fashion brands go minimal in black and white. Choose where on that range the product sits.\n\n<artifact id=\"reqs\" kind=\"requirements\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users preview and synchronize supplied mock purchase-order and receiving records, and review the returned source, vendor, cost, head-count, weight, breed, age, and row-outcome information.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users trace a purchase-order line to one or more lots and lots to pens, and display returned duplicate, pending-mapping, and capacity outcomes while keeping unresolved records unavailable for pen operations.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users view and edit lot target-weight and gain-rate assumptions and display forecasts from incoming animal data, using measured ADG when available and the configured default when it is unavailable.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall provide inventory screens to view feed-item details and enter and review stock movements, including separately entered actual wastage.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-5\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users configure a wastage ratio for consumption estimates and projections and review projected inventory balances for daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, bi-annual, and annual horizons without low-stock alerts.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-6\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users create and view rations with ingredient proportions, nutrition values, intended animal type or weight range, and calculated cost per pound.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall compare feasible ration mixes against user-specified nutritional targets or constraints using recorded ingredient costs and nutrition values, and require an explicit user selection before an optimized suggestion is used.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users generate and review dated feeding plans for specific pens using a selected ration, daily bunk rate, and pen animal count, and display plan status and feeding details.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users record feeding activities for a pen and date with an optional plan link, and require a separate actual quantity for each affected lot in a multi-lot pen.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-10\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall display returned feeding-activity costs, energy, inventory consumption, lot consumption, plan variance, and save or shortage outcomes.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-11\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users record, view, edit, and delete lot weight measurements and display the dated measurement series and returned validation or metric updates.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-12\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall display returned lot feed-to-gain metrics using applicable headcounts at measurement dates and as-fed pounds for feed conversion, and refresh displayed metrics after feeding or weight events are saved, edited, or deleted.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-13\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive web app shall provide the public FeedToGainCalculator.calculate method to calculate days on feed, total lot gain, ADG, as-fed feed pounds, FCR, feed cost per pound of gain, and projected market date from supplied receiving, feeding, weight, headcount, and target-weight data.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-14\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall provide lot- and pen-level dashboards showing returned operational and performance metrics, including best- and worst-performing lots.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-15\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall provide operational report screens comparing pen consumption, lot performance, ration usage, plan alignment, remaining inventory, and feed cost per unit of weight gain.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-16\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall provide a feed-to-gain report comparing lot ADG, FCR, and feed cost per pound of gain, with date-range, breed, sex, and pen filters, and use the date range to select the latest weight reading in range and feeding events up to that reading.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-17\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users export the filtered feed-to-gain report as a CSV file containing its displayed rows and metrics.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-18\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall provide a PDF export action for filtered feed-to-gain report results.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-19\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall let users load a clearly labeled, separate demo dataset on demand and display added, already-present, and failed record outcomes without labeling a partial load complete.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-20\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall provide a project and demo area presenting the proposed solution, architecture diagrams, an app demonstration, testing and validation overview, challenges, production-readiness assessment, and go-to-market analysis.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-21\",\n  \"ears\": \"The responsive feedlot web app shall expose the same product areas, operational records, cost and performance views, import screens, and data-entry controls to all users without role-dependent restrictions.\"\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\nList every screen and write one Stitch prompt for each."}